.resources/TASK-260715-1u2vpc/active-macos-libssh2-viability-matrix-scope.md:1:Execute the accepted candidate-neutral M0-viability matrix for the human-approved libssh2 primary candidate on the current macOS host and SPM harness. iOS is deferred. Do not reopen engine selection, weaken red gates, or invent physical scale/deep-observability numbers. Verify actual selected-adapter behavior: pre-auth host evidence before any credential call; approved authentication through opaque external signer; concurrent direct-tcpip; long-lived exec; exec/stdin upload; client and server rekey-safe traffic; bounded pressure; cancellation and leak-free lifecycle; exact dependency/crypto pins; macOS target integration; privacy and system-domain Keychain query invariants. Use real local sshd/fixture processes and throwaway non-production credential material only; never read or expose user secrets. Record each of the four M3-deferred semantics exactly as reported, not reported, or unsupported and link its owner; physical scale, memory and soak remain later evidence. Produce a reproducible task-scoped matrix with commands, environment, versions, counters and artifacts; any mandatory red row routes focused rework rather than being waived.
.resources/TASK-260715-1u2vpc/owner-approved-m0-vs-release-gate-decoupling.md:1:The owner explicitly decided that Apple A0/account-channel readiness is not a prerequisite for the macOS protocol hypothesis and selected this current Mac for physical validation. TASK-260715-9yp8to is the accepted technical prerequisite: signed provider install/discovery, system VPN approval, versioned message, repeated lifecycle and clean stop passed on the current Apple-silicon Mac. The missing organization membership/agreement/admin evidence in TASK-260715-2ayxqn remains a release/account-readiness gate only and must not block this M0 libssh2 functional matrix. iOS remains deferred. Do not infer release readiness or alter the P0 disposition; only execute and report the macOS protocol/adapter viability rows.
.resources/TASK-260715-28ok1k/TASK-260715-28ok1k_audit-scope.md:1:Pin + audit the two SSH engine candidates for the in-extension transport. CANDIDATES: (A) SwiftNIO SSH (apple/swift-nio-ssh) as the ReluxNIOSSH fork basis — pure Swift, embeddable in the NE extension, but upstream states it is BUILDING BLOCKS not a production client and supports only modern algorithms (Ed25519/ECDSA host keys, AES-GCM, curve25519/x25519); (B) libssh2 — wider algorithm set + explicit channel-window control, C library. Per .spec/ssh-transport.md and ADR-005 (direct-tcpip for TCP + exec/stdio relay for UDP), ADR-006 (upload via exec stdin, NO SFTP), ADR-014 (engine selection is OPEN, gated on this audit + later tests), ADR-019 (ReluxNIOSSH stays a SOURCE SwiftPM dep; libssh2 packaged as a static XCFramework binaryTarget via the 1g9cyt native seam). AUDIT each candidate: (1) pin exact version/commit + license (NIOSSH Apache-2.0, libssh2 BSD — confirm + notices); (2) API surface for direct-tcpip channels, exec channels, and stdin upload; (3) algorithm coverage — kex, cipher, MAC, host-key — measured against real-world OpenSSH server defaults incl. whether RSA host keys / older servers are reachable; (4) PER-CHANNEL WINDOW control (go/no-go for the memory budget — can window size be set/observed per channel?); (5) REKEY support and behavior under sustained multi-GB transfer (go/no-go — VPN traffic forces rekey; note any known failure); (6) concurrency headroom (target 500-1000 simultaneous channels) and per-channel/base memory footprint; (7) build/embedding path (NIOSSH SwiftPM source vs libssh2 XCFramework). Deliver a dated TASK-ID-scoped comparative audit usable by TASK-260715-2ny6z4 (conformance contract), nzdzv3 (NIOSSH fork), 1af33i/1ozsb6 (adapters). Do NOT select the engine here (that is TASK-260715-1gjxer, gated on functional/rekey/scale tests + physical evidence); audit both fairly. If a claim cannot be verified from source/docs, note the gap; do not guess.
